Actually, I should be more careful here.  For classification trees, I think
what I said above is true.  However, regression trees are quite different
beast.  One way to think about minbucket in regression trees is that it's
sort of like the lower limit on the smoothing parameter in, e.g., kernel
smoothing.  If you don't ever want to see your final tree having a terminal
node with only one data point, you can set minbucket to something that you
feel is more reasonable.  (In Breiman's random forest for regression, that
number is set to 5.)

HTH,
Andy

