Dean,

Your best bet is to literally split the matrix up into the sub
components and do the work without having to worry about the indice
counting. Here is a function that makes use of split() twice to pull it
off. The rudimentary code only allows for square subcomponents.

"subm" <- function(mat, cat)
{ 
 spl <- split(as.data.frame(t(mat)), rep(1:cat, rep(nrow(mat)/cat,
cat)))
 matrix(unlist(lapply(spl, function(el, cat) 
                    lapply(split(t(el), rep(1:cat, rep(nrow(t(el))/cat,
cat))), sum)
        , cat)), nrow = cat)
}

subm(matrix(1:100, nrow = 10), 5)
     [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4] [,5] 
[1,]   26  106  186  266  346
[2,]   34  114  194  274  354
[3,]   42  122  202  282  362
[4,]   50  130  210  290  370
[5,]   58  138  218  298  378

