From timh at insightful.com  Tue Feb 11 18:46:24 2003
From: timh at insightful.com (Tim Hesterberg)
Date: Tue, 11 Feb 2003 10:46:24 -0800
Subject: Re: About_Optimization
In-Reply-To: <D45BC82E21E16149AC5A3F706350626401953AFA@usrymx14.merck.com>
References: <D45BC82E21E16149AC5A3F706350626401953AFA@usrymx14.merck.com>
Message-ID: <200302111846.KAA17020@tomato.insightful.com>

Original query:
> is it possible to optimize the following program:
> 
> x<-rep(0,100)
> y<-rnorm(100)
> z<-rnorm(100)
> for(i in 1:100){
>   x[i]<-length(z[z<y[i]])
> }
> 
> I would like to delete the "for".

That is essentially an empirical cumulative distribution function
calculation, except taking counts instead of fractions.  That is
common enough to merit a "normalize=F" option to cdf() to support it:
        cdf(z, y, normalize=F)
Or, to be more careful about ties:
        100-cdf(-z2, -y, normalize=F)

cdf() is part of the resample library, see
        www.insightful.com/Hesterberg/bootstrap
if you'd like to use this.

This is fast, and scales nicely to larger n.  
Results from sys.time():
        n = 100         n = 10000
loop    .04 .04         38.07 77.88
cdf     .01 .01         .1 .1
outer   .02 .04         gave up after 37.84 214.84 
cut     .01 .01         1.93, 3.62

"outer" is Gunter's suggestion:  colSums(outer(z,y,"<"))
"cut" is Johannesson's suggestion: as.numeric(cut(y,c(-Inf,sort(z),Inf))) - 1

Tim Hesterberg

