Date: Tue, 11 Feb 2003 13:25:00 -0600
Subject: Re: Fisher's protected testing procedure
Message-ID: <A5634E4EAFCBD4119C3200508BC82F7809716214@dama.uthscsa.edu>

Dong,

 

Fisher's LSD test is just the t-test for all pairwise 
comparisons in your ANOVA, but using the variance estimate from the ANOVA rather 
than from only the two groups being compared.  The LSD test can have a 
large Type I error  rate.  The protected procedure is to use the LSD 
test only if the omnibus F-test is significant.  If the F-test is not 
significant, then you declare no significant differences among your 
groups.  At one time and with some justification, the protected procedure 
was thought to control the Type I error rate via the omnibus test and yet have 
good power if you were allowed to proceed to the LSD test.  
However, it is now known that the protected test does not control the Type I 
error rate.  It can no longer be recommended.  See the reference 
below. 

 

@incollection{Zwick93,

Author = {Zwick, R.},

Title = {Pairwise comparison procedures for one-way analysis of variance 
designs},

BookTitle = {A handbook of data analysis in the behavioral 
sciences},

Editor = {Keren, Gideon and Lewis, Charles},

Publisher = {Lawrence Erlbaum Associates},

Address = {Mahwah, NJ},

Pages = {43-71},

Year = {1993} }

 

Joe

