>         I have a newcome question, the function below with only one
> argument, that is at same time the upper limit of the integral and a
> constant in the integrand.
>
> fvalor <- function(alfa)
>          {
>           integrand <- function(tt)
>                {
>                   tt/(alfa*(exp(tt)-1))
>                }
>           valor <- (integrate(integrand, lower = 0, upper = alfa))$integral
>           return(valor)
>          }
>
> When I try to run I receive a message that message (after traceback). It
> seems that for the integrand  alfa is not assigned.

<snip>

The integral call doesn't know where to find `alfa' when it evaluates 
`integrand' in the current frame. You need to assign `alfa' to the local 
frame or, more easily, supply the value of `alfa' to `integrand', as in:

fvalor <- function(alfa) {
  integrand <- function(tt, alfa) {
    tt/(alfa*(exp(tt)-1))
  }
  valor <- integrate(integrand,
                     lower = 0,
                     upper = alfa,
                     alfa = alfa)$integral
  return(valor)
}

OR since alfa is not part of the integral you can also do:

fvalor <- function(alfa) {
  integrand <- function(tt) {
    tt/(exp(tt)-1)
  }
  valor <- integrate(integrand,
                     lower = 0,
                     upper = alfa)$integral/alfa
  return(valor)
}

assuming I did the math correctly in my head.

Regards,
Sundar

